</span><span class="ltx_author_notes">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation">[
</span></span></span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_abstract">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
<p class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have scaled rapidly in size and complexity, requiring increasingly intricate parallelism for distributed training, such as 3D parallelism.
This sophistication motivates a shift toward simpler, more debuggable programming paradigm like Single Program Multiple Data (SPMD).
However, SPMD in eager execution introduces two key challenges: ensuring consistency with single-device execution and achieving high performance at scale.</p>
<p class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>, an eager-mode training system that fully embraces SPMD paradigm to democratize distributed tensor programming.
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> addresses the prevalent issue of inconsistent results in systems like PyTorch by introducing a novel algorithm of distributed Random Number Generation (RNG) compatible with arbitrary sharded operators.
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> also significantly boosts training performance by reducing PyTorch primitive’s overhead and improving communication efficiency.
Evaluations show that <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> delivers up to <math alttext="2.2\times" class="ltx_math_unparsed" display="inline" id="m1"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.2\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over the state-of-the-art training systems, like TorchTitan, and cuts code complexity by <math alttext="78.4\%" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="m2"><semantics><mrow><mn>78.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">78.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>, while preserving single-device-equivalent results.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="p2">
<span class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\checkdata</span>
<p class="ltx_p">[Project Page]<a class="ltx_ref ltx_url" href="https://github.com/volcengine/veScale" title="">https://github.com/volcengine/veScale</a>
<span class="ltx_text" lang="en"></span></p>
</div>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S1" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">The rapid expansion of Large Language Models (LLMs) has brought unprecedented revolutions in numerous areas such as natural language processing and content generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib35" title="">35</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib22" title="">22</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib27" title="">27</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib64" title="">64</a>]</cite>.
The ever-growing LLMs have led to massive computation and memory demands for training, making distributed training an essential solution to meet these requirements.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">Distributed training systems are thus driven to evolve with increasingly complex parallelisms, from a simple data parallel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib25" title="">25</a>]</cite> to the sophisticated N-Dim parallel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib49" title="">49</a>]</cite>.
Although N-Dim parallelism has demonstrated great performance, its programming paradigm has rarely been designed carefully to the extent that model definitions are arbitrarily coupled with distributed system intricacies.
This issue has reached a point where
model developers cannot focus on designing new model structures without having to manage the intricate parallelization across devices.
Meanwhile, they cannot enhance the system performance without hacking new model structures.
This has motivated a new trend in programming paradigms that decouple parallelization from model definitions, enabling independent innovations on model architectures and distributed training systems.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">Decoupled parallelization from model has been explored in prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib24" title="">24</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib1" title="">1</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib29" title="">29</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib26" title="">26</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib69" title="">69</a>]</cite>.
Compiler-based systems like Alpa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib71" title="">71</a>]</cite> and JAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib12" title="">12</a>]</cite> allow users to write programs in a Single-Program Multi-Data (SPMD) style, automatically parallelizing model graphs within the compiler’s passes.
However, these systems are often challenging to use and debug <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib8" title="">8</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib9" title="">9</a>]</cite> compared to eager-based systems that execute model code line-by-line in a script without compilation and offer flexible usage and interactive debug.
Thus, the eager systems like PyTorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib41" title="">41</a>]</cite> dominate the community and own 92% models on HuggingFace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib4" title="">4</a>]</cite>.
Among the eager ecosystem, the widely adopted systems of training LLMs are Megatron-LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib49" title="">49</a>]</cite> and Megatron-DeepSpeed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib47" title="">47</a>]</cite>, due to their notable efficiency and scalability.
Nonetheless, they are also known for the heavily intertwined parallelisms with model definitions.
Fortunately, PyTorch now provides the <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span>istributed<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tensor</span> (<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DTensor</span>) primitive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib62" title="">62</a>]</cite> to enable decoupled parallelization in eager execution.
DTensor supports flexible parallelization by transparently distributing operators from single-device model definitions, and empowers the state-of-the-art training system, TorchTitan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib28" title="">28</a>]</cite>.
Despite these advancements of eager-mode SPMD, our analysis (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Challenges of DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>) identifies two critical issues that remain unaddressed.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p4">
<p class="ltx_p">First, PyTorch DTensor faces a consistency issue in maintaining single-device semantics,
as its parallelization mechanisms do not account for single-device-equivalent randomness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib65" title="">65</a>]</cite>.
This leads to mismatched operations and weight initialization between single-device and distributed execution (as well as between different parallelizations),
resulting in inconsistent training outcomes, which plague reproducibility and debuggability.
Second, DTensor encounters performance issues due to its eager execution, where every operator needs to go through a complex dispatch logic at runtime, leading to significant CPU overhead (e.g., 58% slower than vanilla Tensor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib31" title="">31</a>]</cite>) and costly communication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib32" title="">32</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p5">
<p class="ltx_p">To address all these challenges, this paper presents <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>, an eager-mode distributed training system that fully adopts the SPMD programming, ensuring both <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consistent single-device semantics</span> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high-performance runtime</span> with a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">simple interface</span>.
Our key contributions are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S1.p6">
<p class="ltx_p"><math alttext="\triangleright" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p6.m1"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> requires <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">zero change on model definition</span> for parallelization by offering simple APIs to fully decouple distributed intricacies.
This empowers users to build models as if on a single device, and lets <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> parallelize models transparently while preserving the power of eager for interactive execution and debug in distributed environment.
Our statistics show that <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> saves <math alttext="\sim" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p6.m2"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>78.4% development effort (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S4" title="4 Easy-to-Use API ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S1.p7">
<p class="ltx_p"><math alttext="\triangleright" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p7.m1"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> guarantees consistent single-device semantics by introducing a novel distributed Random Number Generation (RNG) algorithm.
It ensures bit-wise match of arbitrary random tensors between single-device and distributed RNG, regardless of different placements or device counts.
This enables users to compare/reproduce/debug training outcomes reliably, even under randomness throughout distributed training, such as weight initialization and dropout.
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">To the best of our knowledge, such single-device semantic distributed RNG is the first of its kind in the community</span> (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5" title="5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S1.p8">
<p class="ltx_p"><math alttext="\triangleright" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p8.m1"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> identifies the prohibitive CPU overhead incurred by DTensor and eliminates entire overhead through a novel <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Static Eager</span> execution mode as well as three highly effective optimizations.
Additionally, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> improves DTensor communication with fusion across multiple <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parallel dimensions</span> and multiple DTensors into a single operation.
With all optimizations, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> speeds up DTensor by <math alttext="\sim" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p8.m2"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>5.21<math alttext="\times" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p8.m3"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S6" title="6 Performance Optimization ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S1.p9">
<p class="ltx_p"><math alttext="\triangleright" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S1.p9.m1"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> is evaluated with diverse model structures of different sizes, including dense (LLaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib15" title="">15</a>]</cite>), sparse (Mixtral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib21" title="">21</a>]</cite>), and vision models (DiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib42" title="">42</a>]</cite>).
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> achieves <math alttext="\sim 2.2\times" class="ltx_math_unparsed" display="inline" id="S1.p9.m2"><semantics><mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn>2.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 2.2\times</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> end-to-end speedup</span> compared to the state-of-the-art systems like TorchTitan, Megatron-LM, and DeepSpeed, while offering consistent single-device results and requiring minimal development efforts (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S8" title="8 Evaluation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p10">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> has been deployed for internal production
for numerous tasks ranging from pre-training to fine-tuning of LLMs.
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We will release an open source version soon</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S2" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Motivation</h2>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S2.SS1">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Programming Dilemma of Distributed Training</h3>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Fitting modern LLMs on a single device for training becomes infeasible, driving the need for distributed training.
To enable system efficiency, various parallelisms have been developed, including data parallel (DP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib25" title="">25</a>]</cite>, tensor parallel (TP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib49" title="">49</a>]</cite>, sequence parallel (SP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib23" title="">23</a>]</cite>, and optimizer parallel (ZeRO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib46" title="">46</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS1.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">To support these parallelisms but without coupling the model definition, compiler experts developed several distributed programming paradigms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib24" title="">24</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib1" title="">1</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib29" title="">29</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib69" title="">69</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib26" title="">26</a>]</cite>.
Especially, Alpa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib71" title="">71</a>]</cite> and JAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib12" title="">12</a>]</cite> allow users to write SPMD programs and parallelize models based on static model graphs.
However, they often depend on successfully tracing the entire model graph, which is hard to guarantee in practice where non-traceable code are common, such as Python objects, communications, and control flows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib8" title="">8</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib10" title="">10</a>]</cite>.
Also, compiled programs can be hard to debug <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib9" title="">9</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib6" title="">6</a>]</cite>, let alone distributed ones.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS1.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">By contrast, eager-based systems does not rely on tracing nor compilation, offering flexible programming and interactive debugging.
Thus, eager systems like PyTorch dominate 92% models on HuggingFace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib4" title="">4</a>]</cite> and 70% research on PapersWithCode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib66" title="">66</a>]</cite>.
The most adopted eager training system for LLMs are the Megatron-LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib49" title="">49</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib56" title="">56</a>]</cite> family.
However, they are designed for performance instead of easy programming, and require intrusively rewriting the entire model with intertwined distributed logic, heavily burdening the model developers.
For instance, to support TP, users are required to carefully rewrite original model definition’s <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Linear</span>s with <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">RowParallelLinear</span> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">ColumnParallelLinear</span> under multi-device semantics.
Similar efforts also apply to refine <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">LayerNorm</span> for SP and rewrite <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Adam</span> for ZeRO.
It is also users’ responsibility to manually invoke communication on <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">LayerNorm</span>s’ gradient for SP, besides for DP; otherwise, gradients are incorrect.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S2.SS2">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>The Promising DTensor</h3>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S2.F1"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="340" id="S2.F1.g1" src="x1.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">DTensor workflow of executing a sharded matrix multiplication (<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:89%;">mm</span>) on a device. The shaded part in each DTensor indicates the materialized local tensor on this device.
Note for terminology that \raisebox{-0.7pt}{1}⃝ is equal to <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DTensor Communication</span>, and \raisebox{-0.7pt}{2}⃝\raisebox{-0.7pt}{3}⃝\raisebox{-0.7pt}{4}⃝ compose <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DTensor Dispatch</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">PyTorch now offers a promising primitive named DTensor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib62" title="">62</a>]</cite> that provides the opportunity towards decoupled parallelization in eager execution.
It represents a global tensor distributed across a set of devices (referred to as a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">device mesh</span>), where each device in the mesh holds a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">local tensor</span> that represents a shard of the global tensor.
DTensor supports three types of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">placement</span> for sharding a global tensor: <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Shard</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">dim</span>)</span></span>, <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Replicate</span>, and <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span>.
It also enables users to switch between these placement via <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">redistribution</span>, which is achieved by implicitly triggering collective communications.
Additionally, DTensors can be computed directly by operators, where each operator owns valid sharding strategies that describe expected input and output placement.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS2.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 The Promising DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the workflow of DTensor.
\raisebox{-0.7pt}{1}⃝ First, the input DTensor is <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">redistributed</span> from its source placement to the expected placement for a valid sharded operation, e.g., column-wise matrix multiply.
\raisebox{-0.7pt}{2}⃝ Next, the sharding metadata of DTensors (placement, global shape, global stride, etc.) is <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">propagated</span> from input to output by performing an metadata inference on the CPU, generating the output’s metadata.
\raisebox{-0.7pt}{3}⃝ Then the local tensors are extracted from input DTensors and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">executed</span> by the operator to produce local output on each GPU device.
\raisebox{-0.7pt}{4}⃝ Finally, each local output tensor, along with its global output metadata, is <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wrapped</span> back into a DTensor.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_table" id="S2.T1">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mismatched model weights between single-device and distributed initialization on eight devices, shown in terms of element-wise maximal and mean difference. Weights are initialized with <math alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,0.025)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.T1.m2"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.025</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,0.025)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
<table class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr class="ltx_tr">
<td class="ltx_td ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="2"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">TorchTitan (DTensor)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="2"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Megatron-LM</span></td>
</tr>
<tr class="ltx_tr">
<td class="ltx_td ltx_border_l ltx_border_r"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Max</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Mean</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Max</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Mean</span></td>
</tr>
<tr class="ltx_tr">
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Llama-3 8B</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">0.1819</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.0197</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">0.1632</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.0197</span></td>
</tr>
<tr class="ltx_tr">
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Mixtral 8x7B</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">0.1948</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.0197</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">0.1818</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.0197</span></td>
</tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S2.SS3">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Challenges of DTensor</h3>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Despite DTensor’s success in enabling parallelization, it faces critical challenges in consistent semantics and performance.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S2.SS3.p2">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Poor Consistency.  </span>
Ensuring consistent semantics during parallelization is essential for large-scale distributed training and debugging.
Following the single-device programming paradigm, we argue that the consistent semantics of DTensor is <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single-device semantics</span>, meaning that <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">merging all local tensors across devices should yield the exact same global tensor on a single device</span>.
For example, local tensors are <math alttext="[1,2]" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.SS3.p2.m1"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> on GPU 0 and <math alttext="[3,4]" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.SS3.p2.m2"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[3,4]</annotation></semantics></math> on GPU 1, which can be merged to match the global tensor <math alttext="[1,2,3,4]" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.SS3.p2.m3"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2,3,4]</annotation></semantics></math> on a single GPU.
However, the single-device semantics can be easily violated due to undesirable RNG designs or completely missing the fundamental for distributed RNG, e.g., random tensors are generated as <math alttext="[1,2]" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.SS3.p2.m4"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> on GPU 0 and <math alttext="[5,6]" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.SS3.p2.m5"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[5,6]</annotation></semantics></math> on GPU 1, which does not match the global random tensor <math alttext="[1,2,3,4]" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.SS3.p2.m6"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2,3,4]</annotation></semantics></math> on a single GPU.
We observed that violating this semantics can produce severe consequences, as minor numerical inconsistencies can be amplified across varying device counts, potentially degrading training accuracy or, in the worst case, causing model divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib20" title="">20</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib51" title="">51</a>]</cite>.
Such inconsistencies are extremely difficult to diagnose in production environments, substantially complicating both debugging and reproducing distributed training at scale.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS3.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, the current DTensor does not adhere to single-device semantics, which is even deemed <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">impossible</span> to achieve <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib65" title="">65</a>]</cite>.
Table <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 The Promising DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares weight initialization using DTensor and single device, where large difference is observed in <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">four orders of magnitudes</span>.
Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.F2.sf1" title="Figure 2(a) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ 2.3 Challenges of DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> further compares training loss curves of LLaMA 7B, using correct weight initialization, the same random states, and the same hyper-parameters; however, a non-trivial difference is still observed.
This mismatch is not only observed in DTensor but also recognized in most systems such as Megatron-LM (shown in Table <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 The Promising DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.F2.sf1" title="Figure 2(a) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ 2.3 Challenges of DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>) and DeepSpeed (e.g., an open issue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib43" title="">43</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S2.F2">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center" id="S2.F2.sf1"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" height="830" id="S2.F2.sf1.g1" src="x2.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Loss comparison between eight devices with single device.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div class="ltx_flex_break"></div>
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center" id="S2.F2.sf2"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" height="830" id="S2.F2.sf2.g1" src="x3.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">DTensor overhead in end-to-end throughput on 32 devices.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training loss and performance using DTensor.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S2.SS3.p4">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Poor Performance.  </span> DTensor also suffers from poor performance.
Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.F2.sf2" title="Figure 2(b) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ 2.3 Challenges of DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a> compares end-to-end training performance between TorchTitan with DTensors and local-tensor training without DTensor.
We observe that DTensor is significantly slower than local tensors: 35.8% for LLaMA and 61.3% for Mixtral.
Although TorchTitan already attempted to improve by using local-tensor execution for selected model parts, it still suffers from bottlenecks on remaining DTensors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib31" title="">31</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib32" title="">32</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS3.p5">
<p class="ltx_p">We further identify that the poor performance of DTensor is due to two factors: 1) high CPU overhead during dispatch and 2) inefficient communication.
The high CPU overhead arises as every operator needs to go through a complex dispatch logic at runtime (recall Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 The Promising DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), often costing over <math alttext="0.5" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.SS3.p5.m1"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> ms of CPU time, even for fast operators like <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Tensor</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">view</span></span></span>.
Given a model with thousands of operators, the total overhead cannot be hidden by the asynchronous GPU execution, leading to GPU idle.
Also, the DTensor communication accounts for <math alttext="\sim" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S2.SS3.p5.m2"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>11.3% overhead.
Such inefficiency comes from fine-grained communication at per-tensor granularity and per-parallel dimension, failing to fully utilize the bandwidth.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S3" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> Overview</h2>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S3.F3"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" height="693" id="S3.F3.g1" src="x4.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">veScale<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> overview.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we introduce <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>, an eager SPMD system developed on DTensor, delivering consistent semantics and high performance with easy-to-use interfaces.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 veScale Overview ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> provides the overview.
To parallelize model for distributed training, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> takes two inputs:
i) a single-device model definition of PyTorch without requiring any changes or coupling any parallelism,
and ii) a parallelization Plan that describes parallel strategies, such as how to shard weights (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S4" title="4 Easy-to-Use API ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Note that the Plan is orthogonal to the model and can be reused across different models/layers via a Plan Zoo.
Next, the Plan is transformed into the internal Plan IR (Intermediate Representation) without requiring compilation/trace of the model graph, and is further applied onto the model for parallelization.
This process involves replacing weight Tensors with DTensors and generating PyTorch hooks to callback DTensor redistribution at runtime to ensure proper sharding specified by the Plan.
At runtime, the training script runs DTensors in eager execution with:
i) a consistent distributed RNG to ensure single-device semantics for all randomness (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5" title="5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>),
ii) an efficient DTensor dispatch to minimize its CPU overhead (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Efficient DTensor Dispatch ‣ 6 Performance Optimization ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> and §<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S6.SS2" title="6.2 Achieving Zero Dispatch Overhead ‣ 6 Performance Optimization ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>),
and iii) an optimized DTensor communication to improve bandwidth utilization (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Optimized DTensor Communication ‣ 6 Performance Optimization ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S4" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Easy-to-Use API</h2>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Easy-to-Use API ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows two APIs of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>: <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Plan API</span> specifies N-Dim parallel decoupled from a model definition and generates a Plan, and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Main API</span> applies the Plan for execution.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S4.p2">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Plan API.  </span>
The <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">VescalePlan</span> API enables flexible parallelisms through descriptive specification of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> to shard <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which</span> tensor and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">when</span>: <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">shard</span>(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">tensor_path</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">placement</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">mesh</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">phase</span>)</span></span>.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S4.F4"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" height="880" id="S4.F4.g1" src="x5.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">veScale<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> API for eager SPMD.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Easy-to-Use API ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, this API precisely targets <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which</span> tensor via <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">tensor_path</span>.
This is achieved by leveraging the built-in Module hierarchy of a PyTorch model definition (e.g., nested <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">nn</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Module</span></span></span> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">nn</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Linear</span></span></span> with a path <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">blk1</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">fc1</span></span></span>) plus the tensor name (e.g., a parameter named <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">weight</span> and input activation denoted as <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword">in</span>&gt;</span>).
This API also supports regular expressions to match multiple tensors (e.g., <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">blk</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">\<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">d</span>+</span></span> for all <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">blk</span>s), which is essential for ease-of-use when working on repeated model structures like LLMs.
Upon targeting tensor(s), this API can specify <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how</span> to shard with desired <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">placement</span> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">mesh</span> for flexible parallelism.
E.g., Megatron-LM-style tensor parallel can be specified by sharding <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">fc1</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword">in</span>&gt;</span></span> to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Replicate</span>, <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">fc1</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">weight</span></span></span> to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Shard</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">(1)</span></span>, and <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">fc2</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">weight</span></span></span> to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Shard</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">(0)</span></span>, with all on a (sub) device mesh named <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">TP</span>.
Specifying sequence or data parallel is similar, except that data parallel is often placed on another sub device mesh (e.g., <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">DP</span>) or another dimension of the N-Dim device mesh.
Furthermore, rather than fixing the sharding across different time phases of training, this API also allows specifying <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">when</span> to shard by separating different placements at different <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">phase</span>s, enabling advanced parallels like FSDP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib70" title="">70</a>]</cite>/ZeRO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib46" title="">46</a>]</cite>.
E.g., ZeRO-3 can be specified by sharding all weights to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Shard</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">(0)</span></span> at initialization (<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">phase</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">=<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">INIT</span></span></span>) and then resharding to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Replicate</span> at forward/backward (<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">phase</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">=<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">RUN</span></span></span>).
Lastly, this API does not demand specifying sharding of gradients, as it is handled automatically under the hood with auto-differentiable hooks.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S4.F5"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="395" id="S4.F5.g1" src="x6.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of RNG designs with a simple example of generating random numbers <math alttext="[7,2,1,9]" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.F5.m2"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[7,2,1,9]</annotation></semantics></math>. (a) is out-dated but still assumed by many; (b) is used by PyTorch on a single GPU; (c) is the goal to achieve; (d) is used by most works (Megatron-LM, DeepSpeed, and JAX); (e) is used by TorchTitan; (f) is proposed by <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> for <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single-device-semantic distributed RNG</span>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S4.p4">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main API.  </span>
The <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">parallelize</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">model</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">plan</span>)</span></span> API applies <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span> onto unchanged <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">model</span> to initialize an N-Dim parallelized model (recall Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 veScale Overview ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
In this process, Plan IR takes the key role for transformation and optimization of the Plan logic.
E.g., the first <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">shard</span></span></span> in Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Easy-to-Use API ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is lowered into an IR abbreviated as <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">blk1</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">fc1</span>.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">forward_pre</span>:&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword">in</span>&gt;:0:<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">redist</span>(-&gt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">R</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">TP</span>)</span></span>.
Especially, the IR transformation not only resolves regex path (<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">blk1</span>), but also translates <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">shard</span></span></span> to representation of a correct PyTorch hook (<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">forward_pre</span>) and a desired hook function for DTensor redistribution (<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">redist</span>).
Then the IR optimization <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fuses multiple IRs</span> with the same path and hook into one, and further batches their hook functions with <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">duplication</span> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">valid reordering</span>.
Afterwards, the optimized IRs are converted into real hooks which are installed on the model with callback execution at runtime with minimal overhead.
Meanwhile, the IR transformation also figures out when hooks are not needed, such as the parameter sharding or <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">INIT</span> phase, and directly converts them into DTensors.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.p5">
<p class="ltx_p">After <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">parallelize</span>, the optimizer is also parallelized automatically by just using a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PyTorch-native optimizer</span> taking DTensor weights of the model, which again decouples the heavy complexity of optimizer parallel like Megatron-LM’s DistributedOptimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib37" title="">37</a>]</cite>.
Now, with both parallelized model and optimizer, users can enjoy parallel training with only a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single-device script</span>, and let <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> transparently handle all runtime complexity (consistency and performance).
Further, empowered by eager execution, users can even <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interactively debug</span> the runtime with arbitrary breakpoints and line-by-line steps.
To achieve this in distributed environment, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> extends PDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib44" title="">44</a>]</cite> and offers <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">breakpoint</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">()</span></span> API.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S5" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Consistent Semantics</h2>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">This section explains how <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> achieves consistent single-device semantics of SPMD training.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S5.p2">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Root Cause.  </span>
Upon investigating the misaligned single-device semantics in existing systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib49" title="">49</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib47" title="">47</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib13" title="">13</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib28" title="">28</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib26" title="">26</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib43" title="">43</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib12" title="">12</a>]</cite>, we identify the root cause as misaligned distributed <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">R</span>andom <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">N</span>umber <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">G</span>eneration (<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RNG</span>).
Specifically, the distributed RNG result does not match the original RNG result on a single device (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Challenges of DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>). Although existing works have attempted simple seeding or offsetting of the RNG state between devices, these solutions are still insufficient to achieve the <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single-device-semantic</span> RNG.
Furthermore, inconsistent distributed RNG impacts the training from end to end, such as in distributed weight initialization, distributed data sampling, and distributed operators like dropout, thus compromising the single-device semantics of the entire training.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Easy-to-Use API ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarizes all existing RNG designs.
A common but outdated concept of RNG is shown in <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> where a random sequence is generated sequentially from an initial seed and is stepped by element-wise offset.
However, modern designs utilize a GPU for parallel RNG <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> where a random sequence is generated in parallel with each thread rendering an element under the same global seed and the same global offset, and the RNG continues with all threads stepping to the next global offset. Hence, each random element is determined by three factors: <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">seed</span>, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">offset</span>, and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">thread index</span>.
When scaling up to distributed RNG <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span>, the original random sequence is sharded across multiple GPUs, where each GPU generates a subsequence but all GPUs together generate the original sequence on the single GPU, which is the desired result.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.p4">
<p class="ltx_p">Regrettably, most works opt to parallelize the seed <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(d)</span>, assigning each GPU a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">different local seed</span> (typically incremented by each GPU’s rank <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib48" title="">48</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib45" title="">45</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib5" title="">5</a>]</cite>).
This leads to subsequences that are <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">statistically uncorrelated</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib39" title="">39</a>]</cite> and mismatch the single-device result.
Other works, such as TorchTitan, parallelize the offset <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(e)</span>, assigning a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">different local offset</span> (usually incremented by local subsequence size <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib11" title="">11</a>]</cite>) to each GPU for generating its subsequence.
This is equivalent to non-contiguously sample numbers in the original random sequence, thus still mismatching the single-device result.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.p5">
<p class="ltx_p">In this work, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> proposes a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">thread-based</span> distributed RNG design <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(f)</span>, where threads and offsets are virtualized as if operating within a global virtual GPU. Each local thread on a physical GPU is mapped to a virtual thread, generating the desired random subsequence as if it were from the global virtual GPU. Thus, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> ensures single-device semantics for distributed RNG and SPMD training.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S5.F6"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" height="910" id="S5.F6.g1" src="x7.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">veScale<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">’s single-device semantic distributed RNG supporting <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">arbitrary tensor dimensions, sizes, and both contiguous and non-contiguous sharding</span>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S5.p6">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-Device Semantic Distributed RNG.  </span>
Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates two examples of generating distributed random tensors with single-device semantics.
The flow is as below:</p>
<ol class="ltx_enumerate" id="S5.I1">
<li class="ltx_item" id="S5.I1.i1" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.I1.i1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">A distributed random tensor is defined with arbitrary dimension/size and is arbitrarily sharded across GPUs.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S5.I1.i2" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.I1.i2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">The tensor is flatten in memory (global view) and can be contiguous (a) or non-contiguous (b).</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S5.I1.i3" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.I1.i3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Each GPU holds a local tensor that is also flattened in memory and is folded against number of local threads.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S5.I1.i4" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.I1.i4.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Each local thread wants to generate a random value to fill an element in the local tensor at index <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S5.I1.i5" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.I1.i5.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">To do so, local index <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m1"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is first mapped to the global index <math alttext="j" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m2"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in the distributed tensor, and then <math alttext="j" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m3"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to the global virtual thread and offset.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S5.I1.i6" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.I1.i6.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">With virtual thread and offset plus a constant global seed, a backend random value generator (e.g., <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">curand</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib39" title="">39</a>]</cite>) is invoked to generate a random value for the element at <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S5.I1.i6.p1.m1"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S5.I1.i7" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">7.</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.I1.i7.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Each local thread then advances to the next local index by the number of local threads and repeats the step 4.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S5.I1.i8" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">8.</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S5.I1.i8.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">All local tensors are generated concurrently on their GPUs with possibly different virtual threads and offsets.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
<p class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> details the formal flow (with simplification).</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" id="alg1">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>’s Single-device-semantic distributed RNG.</figcaption>
<div class="ltx_listing ltx_listing">
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l1">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;color:#000080;">// A parallel algorithm in per-thread view on local GPU</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l2">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Input:</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> local tensor flattened in memory </span><math alttext="\chi" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l2.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">,
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l3">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">      local thread index </span><math alttext="\tau" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l3.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">,
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l4">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">      global random seed </span><math alttext="\alpha" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l4.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> and global offset </span><math alttext="\beta" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l4.m2"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">,
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l5">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">      </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;color:#000080;">// Meta data below; got from DTensor and GPU</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l6">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">      local tensor shape </span><math alttext="\upsilon" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l6.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">υ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">,
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l7">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">      global tensor shape </span><math alttext="\omega" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l7.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> and stride </span><math alttext="\pi" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l7.m2"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">,
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l8">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">      local tensor coordinate in global tensor </span><math alttext="\rho" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l8.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">,
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l9">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">      local thread count </span><math alttext="\theta" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l9.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> and global thread count </span><math alttext="\Theta" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l9.m2"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">,
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l10">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">      local random value generator </span><math alttext="\lambda" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l10.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> (e.g., </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">curand</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">)
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l11">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Output:</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> local tensor filled with desired random values </span><math alttext="\chi" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l11.m1"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l12">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  Get dimension count </span><math alttext="D\leftarrow|\upsilon|" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l12.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">υ</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\leftarrow|\upsilon|</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l13">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  Get local element count </span><math alttext="I\leftarrow|\chi|" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l13.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">χ</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\leftarrow|\chi|</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l14">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;color:#000080;">// Generate random value for local tensor element at <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l14.m1"><semantics><mi mathcolor="#000080">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l15">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">for</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><math alttext="i\leftarrow\tau" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l15.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">, </span><math alttext="i\leftarrow i+\theta" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l15.m2"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow i+\theta</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">, while </span><math alttext="i&lt;\left\lceil I/\theta\right\rceil\theta" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l15.m3"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">θ</mi></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\left\lceil I/\theta\right\rceil\theta</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">do</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l16">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">if</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><math alttext="i&lt;I" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l16.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;I</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">then</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l17">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;color:#000080;">// Map local index <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l17.m1"><semantics><mi mathcolor="#000080">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to global index <math alttext="j" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l17.m2"><semantics><mi mathcolor="#000080">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in flattened memory</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l18">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><math alttext="i^{\prime}\leftarrow i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l18.m1"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}\leftarrow i</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l19">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><math alttext="j\leftarrow 0" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l19.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l20">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">for</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><math alttext="d\leftarrow D-1" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l20.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">D</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leftarrow D-1</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">to</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><math alttext="0" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l20.m2"><mn mathsize="0.800em">0</mn></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">do</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l21">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">     Get coordinate </span><math alttext="c_{d}\leftarrow\rho_{d}+i^{\prime}\bmod\upsilon_{d}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l21.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">ρ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">mod</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">υ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{d}\leftarrow\rho_{d}+i^{\prime}\bmod\upsilon_{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l22">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">     </span><math alttext="j\leftarrow j+c_{d}\cdot\pi_{d}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l22.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">π</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leftarrow j+c_{d}\cdot\pi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l23">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">     </span><math alttext="i^{\prime}\leftarrow\left\lfloor i^{\prime}/\upsilon_{d}\right\rfloor" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l23.m1"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">′</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">υ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}\leftarrow\left\lfloor i^{\prime}/\upsilon_{d}\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l24">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">end</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">for</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l25">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;color:#000080;">// Virtualize thread index and offset</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l26">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><math alttext="\tau_{virtual}\leftarrow j\bmod\Theta" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l26.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">τ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">mod</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{virtual}\leftarrow j\bmod\Theta</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l27">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><math alttext="\beta_{virtual}\leftarrow\left\lfloor j/\Theta\right\rfloor" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l27.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">j</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{virtual}\leftarrow\left\lfloor j/\Theta\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l28">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;color:#000080;">// Generate random value <math alttext="r" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l28.m1"><semantics><mi mathcolor="#000080">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in global view</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l29">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><math alttext="\beta_{virtual}\leftarrow\beta_{virtual}+\beta" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l29.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{virtual}\leftarrow\beta_{virtual}+\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l30">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><math alttext="r\leftarrow\lambda(\alpha,\tau_{virtual},\beta_{virtual})" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l30.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">τ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">β</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leftarrow\lambda(\alpha,\tau_{virtual},\beta_{virtual})</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l31">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><math alttext="\chi_{i}\leftarrow r" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="alg1.l31.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">χ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{i}\leftarrow r</annotation></semantics></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l32">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">end</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">if</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_listingline" id="alg1.l33">
<span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">end</span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S5.F7"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="164" id="S5.F7.g1" src="x8.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">DTensor dispatch process (shown in “blue”) with <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>’s three performance optimizations (shown in “green”). Illustrated by a simplified example of sharded matrix multiplication (<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:89%;">mm</span>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S6" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Performance Optimization</h2>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">This section introduces the primary techniques developed by <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> to achieve high end-to-end performance.</p>
</div>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S6.SS1">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Efficient DTensor Dispatch</h3>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Slow DTensor Dispatch.  </span>
As discussed in §<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Challenges of DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, DTensor incurs significant CPU overhead compared with local Tensor.
We found the root cause to be the <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DTensor Dispatch</span> process, which involves not just local Tensor execution but also complex computation to infer the metadata of output DTensor(s) (including placement, global shape, global stride, etc.).
Such inferring requires parsing the incoming operator and all its inputs, and then propagates the metadata from input DTensors to output DTensor(s) – <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sharding Propagation</span>.
Only with the inferred metadata and local output tensor, the output DTensor can be produced.
Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows a simplified DTensor dispatch.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.SS1.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">We observe that Sharding Propagation is the major overhead (up to 580 <math alttext="\mu" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p2.m1"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s per operator dispatch) and is at least 7<math alttext="\times" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p2.m2"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> longer than local tensor execution (i.e., the real work).
This observation is consistent with findings in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib31" title="">31</a>]</cite>.
To be specific, Sharding Propagation is heavily plagued by inferring shape/stride using Fake Tensors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib59" title="">59</a>]</cite> that contain only metadata without real tensor data.
Such inferring runs operators on CPU with input Fake Tensors to produce output Fake Tensor for using its shape/stride.
It is deemed as <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pretty slow in practice</span> by the PyTorch officials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib60" title="">60</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.SS1.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">To reduce the unbearable tax of DTensor dispatch, we introduce three effective optimizations,
as shown in Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS1.p4">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">\raisebox{-0.7pt}{1}⃝ Rule-based Bypass.  </span>
It is motivated by the fact that certain operators have their output metadata already known, e.g.,
<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">mm</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">a</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">b</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">out</span>)</span></span> with a given output placeholder containing metadata or <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">equal</span> with a boolean output without metadata, which allows us to bypass DTensor dispatch entirely.
Inspired by network rules, we designed <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DTensor Dispatch Rules</span> to match and act as bypassing the dispatch at entry or halfway.
Incoming operators and input DTensors are matched against our rules that are defined by four fields: <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">op</span>-<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">name</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">op</span>-<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">kind</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_lst_keywords2">input</span>-<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">placement</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">custom</span>-<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">meta</span>&gt;</span>.
E.g., a rule:</p>
<ul class="ltx_itemize" id="S6.I1">
<li class="ltx_item" id="S6.I1.i1" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.I1.i1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">equal</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*&gt;</span> captures <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">equal</span> operator and applies its bypass logic at entry, i.e., fast comparison on two local Tensors with a boolean output, rather than go through Sharding Propagation for slow comparison on two DTensors with unnecessary inference overhead for output metadata.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S6.I1.i2" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.I1.i2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">&lt;*,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">out</span>-<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">given</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*&gt;</span> captures any operators with output tensor already given (placeholder to fill values), such as <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">mm</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">a</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">b</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">out</span>=<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">c</span>)</span></span> in Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, and directly uses <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">c</span>’s metadata.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S6.I1.i3" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.I1.i3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">&lt;*,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">out</span>-<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">partial</span>&gt;</span> captures any operators with output placement known to be <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span>, i.e., in half-way of Sharding Propagation, and bypasses the rest inference and uses produced local output’s metadata plus <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span>.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S6.I1.i4" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.I1.i4.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">add</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">partial</span>-<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">replicate</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>*&gt;</span> captures <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">add</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">a</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">b</span>)</span></span> operator with <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">a</span> in <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">b</span> in <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Replicate</span> and applies <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">communication-free</span> add, i.e., convert <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">b</span> into <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span> by zero-out <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">b</span> on non-first devices, and then add legally with <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">a</span>, which would otherwise requires an AllReduce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib61" title="">61</a>]</cite> to communicate <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">a</span> into <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Replicate</span> before adding legally with <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">b</span>.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="ltx_p">All rules follow <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">first-match</span> principle and are ordered properly, and they can also be extended or customized by users.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS1.p5">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">\raisebox{-0.7pt}{2}⃝ Sharding Propagation Cache.  </span>
We employ a caching mechanism that tracks the all operators and their input metadata.
This allows <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> to reuse previous inferred results and skip the rest of Sharding Propagation.
Although native DTensor dispatch has caching, its hashing overhead is too prohibitive, due to its high complexity of <math alttext="O(M\cdot E)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p5.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(M\cdot E)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math alttext="M" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p5.m2"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is number of metadata and <math alttext="E" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p5.m3"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is number of operands.
Therefore, we develop a light-weight hashing to minimize <math alttext="M" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p5.m4"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math alttext="E" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p5.m5"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> while still avoid collision.
Furthermore, although cache misses do happen when training with real data of variable sequence lengths, our caching remains effective, as LLMs enjoy repetitive module structures.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS1.p6">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">\raisebox{-0.7pt}{3}⃝ Efficient C++ Core.  </span>
Despite the aforementioned optimizations, there remains 60+ <math alttext="\mu" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p6.m1"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s overhead per dispatch, which stems from essential compute around the cache (unwrapping operands, parsing metadata, computing hash values, etc.).
To further reduce these Python-induced overheads, we implement critical components in C++ for faster Sharding Propagation.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS1.p7">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">\raisebox{-0.7pt}{1}⃝+\raisebox{-0.7pt}{2}⃝+\raisebox{-0.7pt}{3}⃝.  </span>
With all three optimizations, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> cuts around 95% of DTensor dispatch overhead and limits the overhead to approximately 30 <math alttext="\mu" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS1.p7.m1"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s for all operators (see Table 2 in the supplementary material for more details).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S6.SS2">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Achieving Zero Dispatch Overhead</h3>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivation.  </span>
Despite the success of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>’s Eager mode in our production, we encountered scenarios where even minor dispatch overhead per operator is intolerable, particularly in models containing Mixture of Experts (MoE).
Because thousands of lightweight operators can be bounded by total CPU overhead of dispatching instead of the GPU kernel time.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.SS2.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">To achieve zero dispatch overhead, we introduce a new execution mode called <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Static Eager</span> mode.
The key insight is that the metadata of DTensors remain mostly static during runtime.
For example, the weight DTensor remains <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Shard</span> placements throughout tensor parallel training.
The output activation DTensors remain the same placements of input DTensors for element-wise or inplace operators.
In practice, only few critical places really need dynamics in metadata of DTensor, such as the DTensor redistribution (recall Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 The Promising DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) enforcing placement change, or the non-sharded weight gradient with a dynamic placement during backward.
Luckily, these places appear much less frequently compared to the total number of operators in a training iteration.
Therefore, we can treat DTensors’ metadata as static and eliminate the entire DTensor dispatch to avoid the expensive metadata inference.
Only for those dynamic places, we handle them specially.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS2.p3">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static Eager Mode.  </span>
Under this mode, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> discards the DTensor abstraction at runtime and executes all operators directly on local tensors, leaving only <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Local Tensor Execution</span> in Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
For the aforementioned dynamics, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> still avoids DTensors by replacing inferred dynamic metadata with <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pre-described</span> static metadata.
This is achieved via <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>’s Plan API that can pre-describe the metadata, and then at initialization (before runtime) convert them into PyTorch hooks.
At runtime, those hooks are called mechanically to execute same logic as if DTensor still exist.
To be specific:</p>
<ul class="ltx_itemize" id="S6.I2">
<li class="ltx_item" id="S6.I2.i1" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.I2.i1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">redistribute</span>(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">path</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">src</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">dst</span>)</span></span>: facilitates DTensor redistribution of a target tensor with a pre-described
source and destination placement during forward.
For example, <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">redistribute</span>(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">matmul</span>.&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword">in</span>&gt;,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Shard</span>(0),<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Replicate</span>)</span></span> realizes the redistribution stage in Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 The Promising DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Before runtime, the redistribution is lowered into Plan IR, abbreviated as <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">matmul</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">forward_pre</span>:&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword">in</span>&gt;:0:<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">redist</span>(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">S</span>(0)-&gt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">R</span>)</span></span>.
Then the IR is converted into a forward-pre hook registered at <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">matmul</span>.
During forward, the hook is called to execute AllGather communication, redistributing the first input tensor from sharded into replicated.
If needed, the redistribution of gradients during backward can also be implemented by <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">redistribute</span>(...,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">grad_src</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">grad_dst</span>)</span></span>.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S6.I2.i2" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.I2.i2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">annotate</span>(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">path</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">placement</span>)</span></span>: annotates a target local tensor with a pre-described placement, as if it were a DTensor.
For example, <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">annotate</span>(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">weight</span>.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">grad</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Partial</span>)</span></span> labels the gradient of <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">weight</span> (e.g., a replicate weight in sequence parallel) to be <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span> and let it wait for a pending gradient reduction.
It is also lowered into Plan IR and then converted into a backward-post hook which correctly calls gradient reduction.
Furthermore, this API can also annotate activations for certain operators demanding DTensor placement.
E.g., <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">annotate</span>(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">dropout</span>.&lt;<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword">in</span>&gt;,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Shard</span>(0))</span></span> labels the input tensor’s placement for a dropout such that it can trigger the distributed RNG logic (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5" title="5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="ltx_p">Leveraging the Plan APIs above, Static Eager can reproduce the entire training with DTensors in Eager mode but achieve zero dispatch overhead for nearly all operators.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS2.p4">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static Eager Plan Generation.  </span>
While Static Eager improves the overall performance (4.62<math alttext="\times" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS2.p4.m1"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> in §<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S8.SS5" title="8.5 Ablation Study ‣ 8 Evaluation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.5</span></a>), planning <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">redistribute</span> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">annotate</span> demands some knowledge of parallelisms.
To remove this demand, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> can automatically generate these Plans by leveraging warm-up iterations during regular training or a pre-run with Fake Tensors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib59" title="">59</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S6.SS3">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Optimized DTensor Communication</h3>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DTensor Bucketed Gradient Reduce.  </span>
While DTensor provides an elegant abstraction for eager SPMD, it is fundamentally limited by this abstraction in that its communication (DTensor redistribution) must only occur at per-tensor granularity.
This limitation is most dire when meeting gradient reduce, a crucial communication in distributed training that requires reducing weight gradients of entire model across all devices (e.g., AllReduce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib61" title="">61</a>]</cite>).
Because weight gradients are also DTensors, meaning gradient reduction (redistribution from <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span> to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Replicate</span> via AllReduce) must also occur at per-gradient granularity, resulting in numerous small communication pieces.
As known to the network community, such fine-grained communication is detrimental to the bandwidth utilization.
Hence, it is tempting to use the classic DDP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib25" title="">25</a>]</cite> for bucketing multiple gradients together for coarse-grained reduction.
However, these works lack generality and can only handle data parallel training, ignoring other parallelisms that also require gradient reduction, such as sequence parallel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib23" title="">23</a>]</cite> and context parallel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib30" title="">30</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib19" title="">19</a>]</cite> which are essential for LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.SS3.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">Therefore, we develop a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">general gradient reduce that can bucket together DTensors</span> for each parallel strategy (data/sequence/context/etc.), such that all gradient communications are bucketed to boost the bandwidth utilization.
This is achieved by:
1) find DTensors with <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span> placements on the same dimension of the device mesh,
2) extract their local tensors to fill a bucket-sized communication buffer,
3) AllReduce,
4) reverse step 2 and change placements to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Replicate</span>,
and 5) repeat above for all dimensions of device mesh.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S6.F8"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="595" id="S6.F8.g1" src="x9.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">veScale<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">’s N-Dim fused gradient reduce.
Illustrated by a toy example of a single DTensor with <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:89%;">Partial</span> placements on a 2-Dim device mesh of GPUs, where row and column GPUs are for sequence parallel (SP) and data parallel (DP), respectively.
The goal is to communicate each of the local tensors (e.g., <math alttext="0.1\sim 0.4" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.F8.m2"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1\sim 0.4</annotation></semantics></math>) and sum all via AllReduce.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S6.SS3.p3">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">N-Dim Fused Gradient Reduce.  </span>
Beyond bucketing multiple DTensors, we find an another opportunity to further increase the granularity of communication – <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fusing the N-dimensional parallelisms into one</span>.
Specifically, recall each DTensor represents a global Tensor sharded on a N-Dim mesh of devices with each dimension used for one training parallelism.
When comes to gradient DTensor and reduction, the AllReduce must occur at per-dimension granularity, i.e., occur individually along each dimension of the mesh and repeat for all N dimensions.
Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 6.3 Optimized DTensor Communication ‣ 6 Performance Optimization ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>(a) shows a 2-Dim example.
Therefore, we develop a <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">N-Dim gradient reduce that fuses multiple parallel dimensions into one</span> for a single coarse-grained communication, further improving the bandwidth, as shown in Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 6.3 Optimized DTensor Communication ‣ 6 Performance Optimization ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>(b).
This is achieved by:
1) find dimensions of <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Partial</span>,
2) fuse those dimensions into one by flattening the (sub) device mesh,
3) AllReduce on the flatten mesh,
and 4) change placements to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Replicate</span> and mesh to the original one.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_table" id="S6.T2">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Line of Code (LoC) change needed to adapt a single-device training script to 4-Dim parallel training.</span></figcaption>
<div class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:573.4pt;height:145pt;vertical-align:-145.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p class="ltx_p">
<span class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tr">
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2">System</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t ltx_colspan ltx_colspan_4">LLaMA-3</span>
