<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">126</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">4</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">130</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">162</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">4</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">10</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">176</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">82</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">4</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">86</span></span>
<span class="ltx_tr">
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">TorchTitan</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">6</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">57</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">63</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">6</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">79</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">15</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">100</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">8</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">48</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">39</span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">95</span></span>
<span class="ltx_tr">
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">veScale</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">29</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">29</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">38</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">38</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">36</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">38</span></span></span>
</span></p>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S6.SS3.p4">
<p class="ltx_p">Analytically, we validate the benefit of N-Dim fused reduce by adopting the standard cost model of Ring AllReduce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib61" title="">61</a>]</cite>.
The vanilla N-Dim reduce takes the cost of:</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S6.Ex1">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="T_{vanilla}\propto 2SB\cdot\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\frac{P_{i}-1}{P_{i}}\approx 2SB\cdot\textit{\bf N}" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S6.Ex1.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">N</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{vanilla}\propto 2SB\cdot\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\frac{P_{i}-1}{P_{i}}\approx 2SB\cdot\textit{\bf N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="S" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS3.p4.m1"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the size (a local tensor size or bucket size) in byte,
<math alttext="B" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS3.p4.m2"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is transfer time per byte<span class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote" id="footnote1"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span><math alttext="B" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="footnote1.m1"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is roughly similar for intra-node interconnects and inter-node links in modern training clusters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib34" title="">34</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib2" title="">2</a>, <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib40" title="">40</a>]</cite>.
E.g., each GPU in Azure H100 cluster can send message over intra-node NVLinks with 450 GBps per direction and also over inter-node InfiniBand links with 400 GBps (8x400 Gbps) per direction, as modern NCCL network stack can utilize all NICs for each GPU.
</span></span></span>,
<math alttext="N" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS3.p4.m3"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is number of dimensions demanding reduce,
and <math alttext="P_{i}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS3.p4.m4"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is device counts along <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS3.p4.m5"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th dimension<span class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote" id="footnote2"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>The network latency term is omitted as it is negligible compared to bandwidth term <math alttext="B" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="footnote2.m1"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in LLM training where bandwidth and throughput dominate.</span></span></span>.
By contrast, the fused N-Dim reduce costs:</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S6.Ex2">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="T_{fused}\propto 2SB\cdot\frac{\prod_{i=0}^{N-1}P_{i}-1}{\prod_{i=0}^{N-1}P_{i}}\approx 2SB\cdot\textit{\bf 1}" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S6.Ex2.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">1</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{fused}\propto 2SB\cdot\frac{\prod_{i=0}^{N-1}P_{i}-1}{\prod_{i=0}^{N-1}P_{i}}\approx 2SB\cdot\textit{\bf 1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">Thus, for distributed training at scale (large <math alttext="P" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S6.SS3.p4.m6"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>), the vanilla gradient reduce suffers from <math alttext="N\times" class="ltx_math_unparsed" display="inline" id="S6.SS3.p4.m7"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times</annotation></semantics></math> the communication cost while the fused gradient reduce enjoys a cost of only <math alttext="1\times" class="ltx_math_unparsed" display="inline" id="S6.SS3.p4.m8"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S7" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Implementation</h2>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S7.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> is implemented with 76K lines of code (LoC) in Python, 1K LoC in C++, and 300 LoC in CUDA.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S7.p2">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallelized Model.  </span> As discussed in §<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S3" title="3 veScale Overview ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, it is implemented with PyTorch-native hooks on <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">nn</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Module</span></span></span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib57" title="">57</a>]</cite> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">torch</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Tensor</span></span></span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib58" title="">58</a>]</cite> to achieve automatic parallel in eager execution, without intrusively coupling model definition.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S7.p3">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distributed RNG.  </span> Algorithm <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is implemented in CUDA and seamlessly plugged into PyTorch library to support various random operators such that
consistent distributed RNG is invoked transparently at runtime during DTensor dispatching.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S7.p4">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DTensor Extension.  </span> We extend PyTorch DTensor for more functionalities to meet production demands, such as runtime creation of leaf DTensor, a new placement <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">InterleavedShard</span> for non-contiguous shard, and deferred initialization without OOM.
Details can be found in
§ A
in supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S8" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Evaluation</h2>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S8.SS1">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S8.SS1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Environment.  </span>
All experiments were conducted in a cluster of 32 servers with each server equipped with 8 H100-80 GB GPUs, 88 CPU cores, and 1.8 TB CPU memory.
Within each server, GPUs are connected via NVSwitch with 450 GBps bandwidth for GPU-to-GPU communication.
Servers are interconnected using InfiniBand with 8x400 Gbps links.
Software stack uses PyTorch 2.4, NCCL v2.19, and CUDA v12.2.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S8.SS1.p2">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.  </span>
We evaluated three types of large models with dense and sparse structures for language and vision tasks:</p>
<ul class="ltx_itemize" id="S8.I1">
<li class="ltx_item" id="S8.I1.i1" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.I1.i1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLaMA-3</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib15" title="">15</a>]</cite> of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib16" title="">16</a>]</cite> and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">70B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib17" title="">17</a>]</cite>
model size: a dense language model stacked by homogeneous Transformers.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S8.I1.i2" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.I1.i2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mixtral</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib21" title="">21</a>]</cite> of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3B</span> and 56B <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(8<math alttext="\times" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m1"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>7B)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib18" title="">18</a>]</cite> model size: a sparse language model with Mixture-of-Experts (MoE).</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S8.I1.i3" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.I1.i3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LI-DiT</span> of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">10B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib33" title="">33</a>]</cite> model size: a diffusion model consisting of both Convolutions and Transformers.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="ltx_p">All models are trained with Adam optimizer on real datesets with a sequence length of 4K.
The default precision is BF16.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S8.SS1.p3">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.  </span>
We compare with three prominent training systems in the PyTorch ecosystem:</p>
<ul class="ltx_itemize" id="S8.I2">
<li class="ltx_item" id="S8.I2.i1" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.I2.i1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Megatron-LM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib49" title="">49</a>]</cite>: a widely adopted system dedicated for LLM training, known for its efficiency in tensor and sequence parallel but missing optimizer parallel ZeRO-3.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S8.I2.i2" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.I2.i2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Megatron-DeepSpeed</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib56" title="">56</a>]</cite>: an enhanced system on top of the previous one with extra ZeRO-3 of DeepSpeed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib46" title="">46</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S8.I2.i3" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.I2.i3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TorchTitan</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib28" title="">28</a>]</cite>: a popular PyTorch-native system for LLM training, known for its Eager SPMD with DTensors for tensor, sequence, data parallel and ZeRO-3.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="ltx_p">For fair comparison, all baselines and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> share the same 4-Dim parallel: tensor, sequence, data, and ZeRO-3 (except Megatron-LM’s ZeRO-1).
Within tensor and sequence parallel, the specific shardings are aligned to Megatron-LM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S8.SS2">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.2 </span>Development Effort in Lines of Code (LoC)</h3>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.SS2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We measure the development effort needed to adapt a single-device training script for 4-Dim parallel training by how many LoCs are changed.
The single-device scripts are sourced from HuggingFace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib67" title="">67</a>]</cite> for LLaMA and Mixtral, and the official repository for LI-DiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib42" title="">42</a>]</cite>. Table <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.3 Optimized DTensor Communication ‣ 6 Performance Optimization ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares the results.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.SS2.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">First, we observe that <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> achieves <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">almost zero change on all model code</span>
<span class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote" id="footnote3"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>We truthfully report the 2 LoC change in LI-DiT, which is to fix the model’s inconsistent tensor size between initialization and runtime. This inconsistency is hard coded and is to be reported as a potential model bug.</span></span></span>
with fully decoupled distributed logic, thus enjoying simple development of models as if on a single device, without worrying about intricate distributed systems.
By contrast, baselines like Megatron-LM require both heavy and intrusive changes on original model definition for different parallelisms (e.g., <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">ColumnParallelLinear</span> for tensor parallel <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Linear</span>) with up to 162 LoCs, which incurs not only extra effort from system experts but also repeated effort across different models and different parallelisms.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.SS2.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">Second, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> only needs <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">little effort to describe parallelisms</span> in the Plan by leveraging the Plan APIs (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S4" title="4 Easy-to-Use API ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Such effort from users is minimal, e.g., 50% less LoC than TorchTitan that also uses descriptive plans.
The advantages of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> Plan API are threefold:
1) its generality that does not bind a specific parallelism to a certain operator, while TorchTitan plan binds <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">RowwiseParallel</span> to <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Linear</span>, thus failing to support new operators or new parallels unless users develop their own (e.g., MoE operators or hybrid parallel);
2) its automation that does not demand users’ knowledge of source sharding placement nor ask users’ decision if needing Tensor-DTensor conversion at each operator, while TorchTitan plan relies on both due to its inherent optimization using mixed Tensor and DTensor;
and 3) its expressiveness that matches tensors with regular expression patterns, while TorchTitan requires manually traversing a model to check each tensor before describing the plan, even for repeated model structures like LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.SS2.p4">
<p class="ltx_p">Third, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> also requires <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">zero change on its runtime</span> to support diverse model architecture like MoE and Diffusion.
This is not possible without extending the runtime in both Megatron-DeepSpeed and TorchTitan, since the former misses ZeRO-3 support for expert weights and the latter currently lacks tensor parallelism for expert modules.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.SS2.p5">
<p class="ltx_p">Consequently, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> enjoys the minimal development effort among all baselines, saving up to 78.4% total LoC.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S8.F9">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center" id="S8.F9.sf1"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" height="830" id="S8.F9.sf1.g1" src="x10.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Random initialize only</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<div class="ltx_flex_break"></div>
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center" id="S8.F9.sf2"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" height="830" id="S8.F9.sf2.g1" src="x11.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Random dropout only</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">veScale<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">’s single-device semantics in terms of training loss of LLaMA-3-8B with tensor parallel and FP32.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S8.SS3">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.3 </span>Single-Device Semantic</h3>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.SS3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We evaluate the semantics of SPMD training in two cases:</p>
<ul class="ltx_itemize" id="S8.I3">
<li class="ltx_item" id="S8.I3.i1" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.I3.i1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random initialization only</span>: only allow randomness in distributed weight initialization and remove other randomness like dropout operators during training.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S8.I3.i2" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S8.I3.i2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random dropout only</span>: only allow randomness in distributed dropout operators and remove other randomness like weight initialization.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
<p class="ltx_p">For fair comparison, all baselines and <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> share the same initial random state, data sampling, model definition, and training hyper-parameters.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S8.SS3.p2">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Convergence.  </span>
Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S8.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 8.2 Development Effort in Lines of Code (LoC) ‣ 8 Evaluation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> compares training loss of <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> between various tensor parallelism sizes and the single device.
We observe that <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> achieves exact alignment with single-device results across different device numbers in both cases of random initialization and random dropout,
which is attributed to the proposed distributed RNG with virtualized threads (§<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S5" title="5 Consistent Semantics ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
In contrast, all baselines (TorchTitan, Megatron-LM, and Megatron-DeepSpeed) exhibits significant deviations from the single-device result in both cases, up to <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">four orders of magnitudes</span> (recall Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S2.F2.sf1" title="Figure 2(a) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ 2.3 Challenges of DTensor ‣ 2 Motivation ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> and see § B in the supplementary material for more details).
Especially, such deviations vary substantially across different device numbers, leading to <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inconsistent semantics of multi-device execution</span>, let alone single-device semantic.
Such inconsistent semantics root from either seed-based RNG or offset-based RNG with mismatched seeding or offseting in non-first devices (see Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Easy-to-Use API ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(d) and (e)).</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_table" id="S8.T3">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Maximal numerical difference in training loss between distributed and single-device training for LLaMA-3-8B with FP32.</span></figcaption>
