<div class="ltx_para" id="p3">
<span class="ltx_ERROR undefined" lang="en">\beginappendix</span>
</div>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S12" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">12 </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span>’s DTensor Functionality Extension</h2>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S12.p1">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime Creation of Leaf DTensors.  </span>
It is a common practice to create tensors (e.g., calling <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">torch</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">zeros</span></span></span>) directly in  <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Module</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">forward</span>()</span></span> rather than in <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Module</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">__init__</span></span></span>, which miss-es the DTensor semantic (e.g., placement) on those tensors.
This leads to the execution of operators on a mixture of non-converted Tensors and DTensors, thus giving unexpected computation results.
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> develops a new API called <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">plan</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">factory</span>(<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">path</span>,<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">placement</span>)</span></span> and enables conversion of even runtime-created Tensor into DTensor with desired <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">placement</span>, thus resolving this issue.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S12.p2">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">New Placement – </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">InterleavedShard</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.  </span>
The native DTensor placements are insufficient to cover all PyTorch operations.
For instance, operations like <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting"><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">torch</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">.<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">flatten</span></span></span> on a sharded input tensor produce an output tensor that cannot be described by the existing placement types, as each device holds non-contiguous fragments of the global tensor.
Another common scenario occurs in the training of attention layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#bib.bib63" title="">63</a>]</cite> with tensor parallelism, where QKV parameters are fused into a single large parameter for matrix multiplication, resulting in non-contiguous fragments when sharding across attention heads.
To support these cases, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> introduces <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">InterleavedShard</span> placement, which allows for sharding a dimension into smaller chunks arranged in an interleaved pattern.
Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S12.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 12 veScale’s DTensor Functionality Extension ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> shows an example.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S12.F13"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="209" id="S12.F13.g1" src="x15.png" width="830"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>: </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An example of <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:89%;">InterleavedShard</span> usage.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S12.p3">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deferred Initialization  </span>
To support initializing giant models (e.g., with trillions of parameters), <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">veScale</span> offers a <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">deferred_init</span> API for DTensor parameter initialization.
Rather than immediately allocating memory for each DTensor, it records call stacks (e.g., uniform distribution) to each DTensor and delays memory allocation until <span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lst_language_Python ltx_lstlisting ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">parallelize</span>, applying it only to the necessary local tensor on each device.
This approach accelerates initialization and prevents Out-Of-Memory (OOM) issues on both CPU and GPU.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S13" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">13 </span>Baselines’ Semantic in Training Convergence</h2>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S13.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Figure <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S13.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 13 Baselines’ Semantic in Training Convergence ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S13.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ 13 Baselines’ Semantic in Training Convergence ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.07003v1#S13.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ 13 Baselines’ Semantic in Training Convergence ‣ veScale: Consistent and Efficient Tensor Programming with Eager-Mode SPMD"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> show that neither TorchTitan, Megatron-LM, nor Megatron-DeepSpeed achieves consistent semantic for random weight initialization or random dropout in distributed training.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S13.F14">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center" id="S13.F14.sf1"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" height="830" id="S13.F14.sf1.g1" src="x16.png" width="830"/>
